// firebase/firestore.rules.prod
//
// ============================================================================
// THIS IS THE PRODUCTION RULESET. MERGING A CHANGE TO IT DEPLOYS IT (T105).
// ============================================================================
//
// `firebase.json` points its `firestore.rules` key here, and the merge
// workflow (`firebase-hosting-merge.yml`, job `deploy_rules`) deploys it after
// the Cloud Functions and before Hosting. Do not edit the rules in the
// Firebase console: the next merge overwrites the console with this file.
//
// `firestore.rules` beside it is the permissive DEVELOPMENT ruleset -- the
// emulators run without it, from `firebase.emulators.json` -- and is
// deliberately NOT the production policy. Do not "reconcile" the two: its
// blanket `allow read, write: if true` is correct for an emulator and would be
// catastrophic in production.
//
// DEPLOY ORDER: rules go out with the functions they rely on and BEFORE the
// frontend that relies on them. A change that must follow the frontend
// instead (a rule that refuses what the live frontend still writes, as
// 2026-10-04b did) has to ship in two merges: the frontend first, the rule
// in a later one.
//
// Revisions up to 2026-10-04b were pasted into the console by hand; their
// "paste" wording below is history.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-07-29 -- READ BEFORE DEPLOYING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This is the first substantive change to this policy since it was transcribed
// from the console on 2026-07-28. Every claim below was verified by loading
// this file into the Firestore emulator under a scratch project ID and running
// a 17-check battery as a real group admin and a real plain member. The same
// battery ran against the PREVIOUS revision first, so each change is a measured
// difference rather than an assertion.
//
// FOUR CHANGES, in descending order of importance:
//
// 1. RELAXED, AS REQUESTED: a group member may now UPDATE and DELETE campaign
//    content created by any other member. Previously update required
//    `createdBy == uid || isGroupAdmin`, and delete required `isGroupAdmin`
//    outright -- so a member could not even delete an NPC they had created
//    themselves. Meanwhile the app offers Edit and Delete to everyone and has
//    no creator check anywhere in `src/`, so every such attempt failed at write
//    time (bug #1406). This is a shared campaign journal; collective
//    record-keeping is the point, and `createdBy`/`modifiedBy` still record
//    authorship separately, so the audit trail survives -- checked since
//    T132: each is the writer's own id, stamped with the server's time.
//
// 2. SECURITY FIX -- personal notes were readable by the whole group.
//    Notes live at `groups/{groupId}/users/{uid}/notes`, and the previous
//    policy had no rule for them. They were therefore matched by the catch-all
//    `match /{collection}/{docId}/{nestedCollection}/{nestedDocId}`, whose read
//    rule is `isGroupMember(groupId)` -- so ANY member of the group could read,
//    list and write ANY other member's private notes. Verified: all three were
//    ALLOWED under the previous revision. The app never exposes this (the
//    client only ever queries its own uid's path), so nothing leaked through
//    the UI -- but anyone with the Firebase SDK and a group membership could
//    read the lot. Notes are now explicitly owner-only.
//
// 3. SECURITY FIX -- a member could make themselves a group admin. FULLY CLOSED.
//    `allow create, update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId` on
//    the group profile let a member write ANY field of their own profile,
//    including `role`. One `updateDoc({role: "admin"})` and they could delete
//    the group, remove other users, and mint registration tokens. Verified
//    ALLOWED under the previous revision.
//    Now closed on BOTH paths: `update` may no longer touch `role`, and `create`
//    may only ever write `role: "member"`. The second half is possible because
//    group creation moved into the `createGroup` Cloud Function -- see change 5.
//
// 4. SECURITY FIX -- the catch-all wildcard was over-broad on create.
//    `match /{collection}/{docId}` with `allow create: if isGroupMember` let a
//    member create documents in ANY group-level collection, including
//    `registrationTokens` -- i.e. mint their own invitations. Verified ALLOWED
//    under the previous revision. The wildcard is gone; `campaigns` is now
//    matched explicitly, and anything not matched falls through to the final
//    deny. A group's collections are exactly `campaigns`, `users`, `usernames`
//    and `registrationTokens` (confirmed via `listCollectionIds` against the
//    emulator), all four of which have explicit blocks below.
//
// 5. GROUP CREATION MOVED SERVER-SIDE, which is what makes change 3 complete.
//    `GroupService.createGroup` used to run a client-side `runTransaction` that
//    wrote the group document, the caller's global user document, their group
//    profile with `role: 'admin'`, and their username reservation. Because a
//    client had to be allowed to write `role: "admin"` for that to work, the
//    `create` rule could not restrict the role at all -- which left the two-step
//    escalation open however tightly `update` was guarded.
//
//    It now runs in the `createGroup` Cloud Function (`onCall`,
//    `europe-west1`), in an Admin SDK transaction that writes the same four
//    documents with the same field shapes. The Admin SDK bypasses rules, so the
//    creator still becomes an admin while no client is ever permitted to write
//    that value.
//
//    Conditioning the rule on the group document's `createdBy` was considered
//    and rejected instead: the group doc is written in the SAME transaction as
//    the profile, so a rules `get()` may not see committed data, and getting
//    that wrong breaks group creation for everyone.
//
//    ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS. The Cloud Function must be deployed BEFORE this
//    ruleset. Deploying the rules first would reject the old client-side
//    create path and break group creation for anyone on the currently-deployed
//    frontend. Deploy functions, then the frontend, then these rules.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-09-23 -- READ BEFORE DEPLOYING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The console was re-read on 2026-09-23 before this revision and matched the
// previous revision of this file rule for rule, so the 2026-07-29 revision
// above IS live. Every change below is pinned by
// `functions/test/rules/firestore-rules-prod.test.ts`, which loads this file
// into the emulator. Each ALLOWED below was measured against the previous
// revision first.
//
// Membership and administration are now written only by Cloud Functions.
// Every hole closed here had the same shape: the rules trusted a field that
// the caller could write about themselves.
//
// 1. SECURITY FIX (T052) -- anyone could join any group without an invitation.
//    `isGroupMember` trusts `users/{uid}.groups`, and a user could write that
//    array freely. Measured: a stranger listed a group's id in their own
//    profile and could then read and write its campaigns. Group ids leak
//    through every invitation link. Clients may no longer write `groups` at
//    all; joining goes through the `redeemInvitation` Cloud Function, which is
//    also the first place a token's `used` and `expiresAt` are enforced.
//
// 2. SECURITY FIX -- any new account could make itself a GLOBAL admin.
//    `users/{userId}` create checked only the uid, so a fresh account could
//    write `isAdmin: true` into its own profile and then pass `isGlobalAdmin`.
//    Measured: allowed. Clients may no longer create that document at all;
//    `redeemInvitation` and `createGroup` create it with the Admin SDK.
//
// 3. Clients may no longer create their own group profile, reserve a username
//    in a group they are not in, or mark a token used. All three were the
//    client half of the old join; the function does them now.
//
// 4. Roles change only through the `setMemberRole` Cloud Function (T034),
//    which refuses to leave a group without an admin (T035). A rule cannot
//    count admins, so the group-admin update below may no longer touch `role`.
//
// 5. BUG FIX -- a member could not rename themselves. `changeGroupUsername`
//    deletes the old reservation, and only a global admin could delete one.
//    Measured: denied for a plain member. An owner may now delete their own.
//
// ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS, as in 2026-07-29: functions first, then the
// frontend, then these rules. Rules first would break joining for anyone on
// the frontend currently deployed, which still writes membership itself.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-04 -- T084, first half
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One addition, pinned by the "pending uploads" tests in
// `functions/test/rules/firestore-rules-prod.test.ts`:
//
// 6. NEW -- `groups/{groupId}/pendingUploads/{uploadId}`. A member records an
//    image upload before writing the file and deletes the entry once the
//    document points at it, so the daily sweep can tell an upload whose
//    document write is queued in an offline tab from an orphan (IMG-003).
//    Create is for members, in their own name, at the server's time, naming
//    a file in this group under the entry's own id; delete is owner-only.
//
// Deploy order does not matter for this one. Before it is pasted, the client
// finds its entry refused and uploads as it always has; after, the sweep
// spares pending uploads. The second half of T084 -- refusing an image path
// with no live entry, so a write that arrives after the lease cannot point at
// a deleted file -- is a later revision, and THAT one must follow the client.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-04b -- T084, second half -- READ BEFORE DEPLOYING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One change, pinned by the "an image path needs its upload's live entry"
// tests in `functions/test/rules/firestore-rules-prod.test.ts`:
//
// 7. NEW -- a write that points a document at a different image file needs
//    that file's `pendingUploads` entry, naming exactly that path and still
//    within the 30-day lease (`imageLeased` below). It covers an NPC's or a
//    location's `image`, a campaign's `banner` and a group's `crest`. Leaving
//    the image as it is, or removing it, needs nothing. The daily sweep
//    deletes a file once its entry's lease is over, so without this a document
//    write queued for longer than that could still land and point at the
//    deleted file (IMG-003's last case).
//
//    A side effect, on purpose: a write that sends back a stale copy of the
//    whole record (T083) can no longer put back a picture that has since been
//    replaced and deleted. It is refused instead.
//
// ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS: the frontend first, then these rules. A frontend
// that writes no entry (anything before T084's first half) would have every
// image change refused. Paste this once T084's client halves are live, and
// preferably once T083 has stopped edits sending the whole record, so that a
// stale tab's unrelated edit is not refused along with the picture it carries.
//
// LIVE: the maintainer pasted this revision into the console on 2026-10-05,
// after both client halves of T084 and T083's field patches had shipped.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-05 -- T084, the sweep's second ledger
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The first revision deployed by the merge workflow (T105). One addition,
// pinned by the "released images" tests in
// `functions/test/rules/firestore-rules-prod.test.ts`:
//
// 8. NEW -- `groups/{groupId}/releasedImages/{imageId}`. A member records an
//    image file before the write that stops a document pointing at it (a
//    replace, a removal, a deleted NPC or location) and deletes the entry once
//    the file is gone. The daily sweep then reads these entries instead of
//    every document. Same rules as `pendingUploads`.
//
// Deploy order does not matter: the client's record is best-effort, so a
// frontend that ships before the rule finds it refused and carries on, and
// the monthly full sweep still finds what it would have recorded.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-05b -- T037, DATA-010
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One change, pinned by the "a campaign being deleted takes no writes" tests
// in `functions/test/rules/firestore-rules-prod.test.ts`:
//
// 9. NEW -- a campaign must exist and not be marked `deleting` to take a
//    write: its content (create, update), a member's note for it and a
//    member's reading progress in it (create, update). The `deleteCampaign`
//    Cloud Function marks the campaign before its cleanup starts, so a write
//    can no longer land behind the cleanup and survive the deletion under a
//    campaign nobody can open. Requiring the campaign to exist covers the
//    time after its document is gone, offline writes queued before it
//    included. Deletes stay open: removing something never strands it.
//    Clients may not write `deleting` themselves.
//
//    Cost: one more document read per such write (`campaignOpen`); its
//    `exists` and `get` of the same document count once.
//
// Deploy order: the function that sets the mark deploys before these rules,
// as the workflow does. The live frontend never writes into a missing or
// deleting campaign, nor writes `deleting`, so nothing it does is refused.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-05c -- T037, group deletion
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pinned by the "deleting a group is the deleteGroup function's" tests:
//
// 10. CHANGED -- no client deletes a group document. It used to be open to a
//     group admin, and deleting it left every campaign, profile and note
//     beneath it stored and unreachable. The `deleteGroup` Cloud Function
//     deletes the lot.
// 11. NEW -- the group's `deleting` mark, as for campaigns: clients may not
//     write it, and a marked group takes no edits. The function closes the
//     group's content to writes by taking the group off every member's
//     `groups` first, so no further rule is needed for what is beneath it.
//
// Deploy order: the function deploys before these rules, as the workflow
// does. The live frontend never deletes a group document nor writes
// `deleting`, so nothing it does is refused.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-05d -- T088, DATA-006
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pinned by the "a location being deleted takes no new places" tests:
//
// 12. NEW -- a location's `deleting` mark. Deleting a place marks it (and, for
//     a whole subtree, each place inside it, level by level) before finding
//     what is inside it, and a marked place takes no new place, no place
//     moved into it, and no edit. A place created or moved in meanwhile was
//     orphaned when its parent went; now the write is refused. A member may
//     set the mark -- the delete runs in the client -- but only to one of the
//     two ways of deleting, and never clear it. Places inside a marked one
//     may still move out: that is how its children are kept.
//
//     Cost: one more document read for a location written with a parent, or
//     moved to one; none for anything else.
//
// Deploy order does not matter: the live frontend never writes `deleting`
// on a location, and never writes into a marked one, since none exists.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-07 -- T119, the data model review's F4 and F7
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pinned by the "no client creates a group", "a campaign holds the app's
// records" and "the global-admin flag grants nothing" tests. Each was
// measured failing against the previous revision first.
//
// 13. CLOSED -- any signed-in account could create a group document. No
//     client has since 2026-07-29 (`createGroup` does, change 5), so the rule
//     served only to create groups nobody was a member of, without limit.
// 14. NEW -- a campaign holds six kinds of record (`namingField`). A member
//     could write any collection under a campaign; anything else is now
//     refused, reads included. A new record must carry its name (`name` or
//     `title`) as text, and an edit may not remove it or make it something
//     else. A record stored without one can still be edited.
// 15. REMOVED -- the global admin. `users/{uid}.isAdmin` opened every group's
//     content, profiles, names and invitations, and every account's profile,
//     to its holder through the client. Only the maintainer holds it, and
//     they reach the data through the console and the Admin SDK anyway, so
//     it only made their session a key to everything. The `admin`
//     collection, which nothing used, goes with it. `deleteGroup`,
//     `deleteCampaign` and `deleteUser` stop honouring the flag in the same
//     change.
//
// Deploy order does not matter: the live frontend creates no group
// document, writes no other collection under a campaign, gives every new
// record its name as text, and offers nothing that relies on the flag.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-07b -- T119 F4, the size caps -- READ BEFORE MERGING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pinned by the "a record's text has a limit" tests, each measured failing
// against the previous revision first.
//
// 16. NEW -- each text field of a record has a limit (`textFits`), and a
//     campaign's and a group's name and description too (`namedTextFits`):
//     a line 200, a text 10,000, a chapter 200,000, the saga 500,000. A
//     field over its limit, or not text, is refused when a write sets it; a
//     record stored over a limit can still be edited elsewhere. `size()`
//     counts UTF-16 code units, as `String.length` does (measured: two emoji
//     are 4), so text the app lets through is never refused here.
//
// ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS: the frontend that caps the forms (T119's app
// half, `src/core/constants/textLimits.ts`) must be live first. Before it,
// nothing stopped typing at these limits, and a longer text would be
// refused here with "permission denied".
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-11 -- T136, one membership document -- READ BEFORE MERGING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pinned by the "membership is the group profile" tests, each measured
// failing against the previous revision first.
//
// 17. CHANGED -- `isGroupMember` checks that the group profile
//     `groups/{g}/users/{uid}` exists, where it read `users/{uid}.groups`.
//     The profile was already the roster and the role; now it is the one
//     record of membership (docs/architecture/membership-design.md). A check
//     no longer reads the global profile, and a write that checks member and
//     admin reads one document, not two. An account the array lists without
//     a profile is no longer a member; one with a profile the array does not
//     list now is. `scripts/audit-membership.js` lists both, and both should
//     be none before this merges.
//
// ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS: the functions and the app that read the profiles
// (T136 step 2) must be live first.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// KEEPING IT HONEST
// Change the production rules here, in the same PR as whatever motivated the
// change, never in the console. Every merge to `main` redeploys this file, so a
// console edit lasts only until then.
//
// Note the rules are path-scoped, so a `collectionGroup` query is denied
// unless a recursive match (`/{path=**}/...`) allows it. One does: a user's
// own group profiles, for the list of their groups (T136).

rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // Basic helper functions
    function isSignedIn() {
      return request.auth != null;
    }

    // A member is someone with a group profile there, the one record of
    // membership (T136). Only Cloud Functions create or delete one.
    function isGroupMember(groupId) {
      return isSignedIn() &&
             exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/users/$(request.auth.uid));
    }

    // Reads the same document as `isGroupMember`; a request that checks
    // both is billed one read.
    function isGroupAdmin(groupId) {
      let userProfileDoc = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/users/$(request.auth.uid));
      return isSignedIn() && userProfileDoc != null && userProfileDoc.data.role == "admin";
    }

    // The id of a name's reservation: the name lower-cased the way the client
    // does it (`toLowerCase`). The rules' `lower()` folds only ASCII, and
    // usernames also allow Æ, Ø and Å (`UserService.validateGroupUsername`).
    function reservationKey(username) {
      return username.lower().replace('Æ', 'æ').replace('Ø', 'ø').replace('Å', 'å');
    }

    // Whether this write may leave `field` -- an image, `StoredImage` -- as it
    // does (T084). Unchanged or removed needs nothing. Pointing at a different
    // file needs that file's upload entry, below.
    function imageLeased(groupId, field) {
      let after = request.resource.data.get(field, null);
      let before = resource == null ? null : resource.data.get(field, null);
      return after == null || after == before || uploadLeased(groupId, after.path);
    }

    // Whether `path` has a `pendingUploads` entry naming exactly it, inside the
    // 30-day lease after which the daily sweep may delete the file
    // (`PENDING_LEASE_MS` in `functions/src/imageMaintenance/sweepOrphanedImages.ts`).
    function uploadLeased(groupId, path) {
      let segments = path.split('/');
      let entry = /databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/pendingUploads/$(segments[segments.size() - 1]);
      return exists(entry) &&
             get(entry).data.path == path &&
             get(entry).data.createdAt + duration.value(30, 'd') > request.time;
    }

    // Whether a campaign takes writes: it exists and is not being deleted
    // (T037, DATA-010). The `deleteCampaign` function sets `deleting`; a
    // note or reading progress for a campaign it has marked or removed would
    // otherwise survive the deletion.
    function campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) {
      let campaign = /databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/campaigns/$(campaignId);
      return exists(campaign) && get(campaign).data.get('deleting', false) != true;
    }

    // Whether a location may sit inside `parentId` (T088): it names no place,
    // or a place that is not being deleted.
    function parentTakesPlaces(groupId, campaignId, parentId) {
      return parentId == null || parentId == '' ||
             parentOpen(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/campaigns/$(campaignId)/locations/$(parentId));
    }

    // Whether the place at `path`, if there is one, is not being deleted.
    function parentOpen(path) {
      return !exists(path) || get(path).data.get('deleting', null) == null;
    }

    // What a write leaves a location as, against its `deleting` mark (T088).
    // Any other kind of record passes. A marked place takes no edit; the mark
    // is one of the two ways of deleting; a new parent must take places.
    function locationFenceHolds(groupId, campaignId, entityCollection) {
      let after = request.resource.data;
      let parentChanged = resource == null ||
                          after.get('parentId', '') != resource.data.get('parentId', '');
      return entityCollection != 'locations' || (
        (resource == null || resource.data.get('deleting', null) == null) &&
        (resource != null || after.get('deleting', null) == null) &&
        after.get('deleting', null) in [null, 'delete-subtree', 'promote-to-grandparent'] &&
        (!parentChanged || parentTakesPlaces(groupId, campaignId, after.get('parentId', '')))
      );
    }

    // The field that names each kind of record a campaign holds, or null for
    // a collection that is not one (T119). Nothing else under a campaign is
    // read or written.
    function namingField(entityCollection) {
      return {
        'npcs': 'name', 'locations': 'name', 'quests': 'title',
        'rumors': 'title', 'chapters': 'title', 'saga': 'title'
      }.get(entityCollection, null);
    }

    function isRecordType(entityCollection) {
      return namingField(entityCollection) != null;
    }

    // Whether a write leaves the record named (T119): its naming field is
    // text, or the write leaves that field as it was, so a record stored
    // before this check without one can still be edited. The text may be
    // empty: a rumour started from the header is blank.
    function keepsItsName(entityCollection) {
      let field = namingField(entityCollection);
      return request.resource.data.get(field, null) is string ||
             (resource != null &&
              !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny([field]));
    }

    // The records whose notes are documents of their own (T133; quests', T163).
    function hasNotes(entityCollection) {
      return entityCollection in ['npcs', 'locations', 'quests', 'rumors'];
    }

    // A note's text: a person's, place's or quest's note is `text`, a rumour's
    // `content`, each a text of 10,000; its day and author a line (T133).
    function noteTextFits() {
      let changed = changedKeys();
      return fits(changed, 'text', 10000) && fits(changed, 'content', 10000) &&
             fits(changed, 'date', 200) && fits(changed, 'author', 200);
    }

    // A private record carries no picture (T163): a picture's file is stored
    // where every member of the group can read it.
    function holdsNoPicture() {
      return request.resource.data.get('image', null) == null;
    }

    // A held link (T163): its two ends, each a kind and an id, and nothing
    // else. `from` is the private record, `to` the shared one that owns the link.
    function heldLinkShape() {
      let data = request.resource.data;
      let kinds = ['npc', 'location', 'quest', 'rumor'];
      return data.keys().hasOnly(['from', 'to', 'createdAt']) &&
             data.from is map && data.to is map &&
             data.from.keys().hasOnly(['kind', 'id']) && data.to.keys().hasOnly(['kind', 'id']) &&
             data.from.kind in kinds && data.to.kind in kinds &&
             data.from.id is string && data.from.id.size() <= 200 &&
             data.to.id is string && data.to.id.size() <= 200 &&
             data.createdAt == request.time;
    }

    // The fields this write sets or changes: all of them for a new document.
    function changedKeys() {
      return resource == null ? request.resource.data.keys()
                              : request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys();
    }

    // Whether `field` holds at most `limit` characters of text after this
    // write, or is cleared, or is left as it was (T119). `size()` counts as
    // `String.length` does, so the app's limits and these agree exactly.
    function fits(changed, field, limit) {
      let value = request.resource.data.get(field, null);
      return !changed.hasAny([field]) || value == null ||
             (value is string && value.size() <= limit);
    }

    // The text limits of each kind of record (T119): a line 200, a text
    // 10,000, a chapter 200,000, the saga 500,000. The app's
    // `RECORD_TEXT_LIMITS` (src/core/constants/textLimits.ts) holds the same
    // table; change both together. Lists are not checked: no rule can look
    // inside one.
    function textFits(entityCollection) {
      let changed = changedKeys();
      return (entityCollection != 'npcs' || (
               fits(changed, 'name', 200) && fits(changed, 'title', 200) &&
               fits(changed, 'race', 200) && fits(changed, 'occupation', 200) &&
               fits(changed, 'location', 200) && fits(changed, 'description', 10000) &&
               fits(changed, 'appearance', 10000) && fits(changed, 'personality', 10000) &&
               fits(changed, 'background', 10000))) &&
             (entityCollection != 'locations' || (
               fits(changed, 'name', 200) && fits(changed, 'description', 10000))) &&
             (entityCollection != 'quests' || (
               fits(changed, 'title', 200) && fits(changed, 'location', 200) &&
               fits(changed, 'levelRange', 200) && fits(changed, 'description', 10000) &&
               fits(changed, 'background', 10000))) &&
             (entityCollection != 'rumors' || (
               fits(changed, 'title', 200) && fits(changed, 'sourceName', 200) &&
               fits(changed, 'location', 200) && fits(changed, 'content', 10000))) &&
             (entityCollection != 'chapters' || (
               fits(changed, 'title', 200) && fits(changed, 'summary', 10000) &&
               fits(changed, 'content', 200000))) &&
             (entityCollection != 'saga' || (
               fits(changed, 'title', 200) && fits(changed, 'content', 500000)));
    }

    // Who wrote a record or a note, and when, as the rules can check them
    // (T132, F5, F6): the writer's own id, and the server's time of the write.
    // Until these, any member could credit a record to someone else. The
    // names beside the ids (`createdByUsername`, ...) are what the app shows
    // for an author who has left, and are not checked.
    function stampsCreation() {
      let data = request.resource.data;
      return data.get('createdBy', null) == request.auth.uid &&
             data.get('modifiedBy', null) == request.auth.uid &&
             data.get('createdAt', null) == request.time &&
             data.get('modifiedAt', null) == request.time;
    }

    // An edit: by the writer, now, leaving who created it and when as they
    // were -- absent on a record written before either existed.
    function stampsModification() {
      let data = request.resource.data;
      return data.get('createdBy', null) == resource.data.get('createdBy', null) &&
             data.get('createdAt', null) == resource.data.get('createdAt', null) &&
             data.get('modifiedBy', null) == request.auth.uid &&
             data.get('modifiedAt', null) == request.time;
    }

    // A chapter renumbered by another's move or deletion: its place alone
    // changes, and its author's attribution stays (#1203).
    function onlyRenumbersChapter(entityCollection) {
      return entityCollection == 'chapters' && changedKeys().hasOnly(['order']);
    }

    // Whether `field` is left as it was, or emptied: removed, `null`, `''` or
    // `[]`. For the fields below, which nothing reads any more.
    function keptOrEmptied(changed, field) {
      let value = request.resource.data.get(field, null);
      return !changed.hasAny([field]) || value == null || value == '' ||
             (value is list && value.size() == 0);
    }

    // A person's old list of quests, inside `connections`: as it was, or empty.
    function keepsOldQuestList() {
      let after = request.resource.data.get('connections', {}).get('relatedQuests', []);
      let before = resource == null ? [] : resource.data.get('connections', {}).get('relatedQuests', []);
      return after == before || after == null || (after is list && after.size() == 0);
    }

    // The shapes records were moved out of, which nothing reads now: the old
    // second half of a link (T131) -- a person's `locationId` and quest list,
    // a place's quest list -- a record's notes array (T133), a chapter's own
    // text (T134). Production was migrated; a value written there now, by a
    // browser still on the app from before, would vanish from every page, so
    // it is refused instead. The app writes them empty, and a value left as
    // it was stays: a person's `locationId` naming a place since deleted.
    function leavesOldShapesEmpty(entityCollection) {
      let changed = changedKeys();
      return (entityCollection != 'npcs' || (
               keptOrEmptied(changed, 'locationId') && keptOrEmptied(changed, 'notes') &&
               keepsOldQuestList())) &&
             (entityCollection != 'locations' || (
               keptOrEmptied(changed, 'relatedQuests') && keptOrEmptied(changed, 'notes'))) &&
             (entityCollection != 'rumors' || keptOrEmptied(changed, 'notes')) &&
             (entityCollection != 'chapters' || keptOrEmptied(changed, 'content'));
    }

    // A campaign's or a group's name and description (T119), as `createGroup`
    // checks them for a new group.
    function namedTextFits() {
      let changed = changedKeys();
      return fits(changed, 'name', 200) && fits(changed, 'description', 10000);
    }

    // Global user profiles
    match /users/{userId} {
      // Only its holder reads a profile. Until T140 any signed-in account
      // could read one that had no `email`, with its groups, its extraction
      // counters and allowance; no reader needed that, since every app read
      // of `users/{uid}` is the signed-in person's own. What other members
      // see is the group profile, `groups/{g}/users/{uid}`.
      allow read: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId;

      // No client create. This used to be `request.auth.uid == userId` and
      // nothing more, so a fresh account could create its profile with
      // `isAdmin: true` (2026-09-23, change 2). The profile is created by the
      // `redeemInvitation` and `createGroup` Cloud Functions.

      // Users can update only the fields the client itself writes. Every other
      // field is server-owned: `groups` was what `isGroupMember` read, so
      // writing it was joining any group without an invitation (T052), and
      // `entityExtractionUsage` is what the quota reads, so writing it was
      // unlimited paid AI use (T080). An allowlist, so a field added later is
      // server-owned until someone decides otherwise.
      allow update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId &&
                     request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                       .hasOnly(['activeGroupId', 'lastLogin', 'preferences']);

      // No client deletes a profile: the `deleteUser` Cloud Function does,
      // for the account's own holder, with everything beneath it.
    }

    // A user's own group profiles, one per group, across groups: how the app
    // lists the groups someone is in (T136). List only, and only profiles
    // whose server-written `userId` is the reader's. It matches every `users`
    // collection: top-level profiles carry no `userId`, so it grants nothing
    // there, and on a group's roster only a profile of one's own, which exists
    // only for a member. `notes` and `story-progress` sit deeper and do not
    // match.
    match /{path=**}/users/{profileId} {
      allow list: if isSignedIn() && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;
    }

    // Group-level access
    match /groups/{groupId} {
      // Group data is readable by its members
      allow read: if isGroupMember(groupId);

      // No client creates a group: the `createGroup` Cloud Function does,
      // with the creator's admin profile (2026-07-29, change 5). Until T119
      // any signed-in account could still create group documents here that
      // nobody was a member of.
      allow create: if false;

      // Group admins can update -- not once its deletion has started, and
      // never the `deleting` mark (T037).
      allow update: if isGroupAdmin(groupId) && namedTextFits() &&
                     imageLeased(groupId, 'crest') &&
                     resource.data.get('deleting', false) != true &&
                     !request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(['deleting']);

      // No client deletes a group: the `deleteGroup` Cloud Function does,
      // with everything beneath it (T037).
      allow delete: if false;

      // Group user profiles
      match /users/{userId} {
        // Group members can see other group members' profiles
        allow read: if isGroupMember(groupId);

        // No client create. Every group profile is written by a Cloud
        // Function: `createGroup` for the creator (as admin), and
        // `redeemInvitation` for everyone who joins (as member). The previous
        // revision allowed a self-create as "member" for the client-side join,
        // which no longer exists (2026-09-23, change 3).

        // Users can update only the fields the client itself writes on their
        // own group profile. `role` was a one-write escalation to group admin;
        // `userId` and `id` steered an admin's removal onto another member
        // (T080). Neither is in the list, nor anything added later.
        //
        // A new `username` must be reserved to the writer once the write
        // lands -- by the same transaction, as `changeGroupUsername` does, or
        // already (a change of case). Without it a member could take another
        // member's name, and leaving would release that member's reservation.
        allow update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId &&
                       request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                         .hasOnly(['activeCampaignId', 'activeCharacterId', 'characters', 'username']) &&
                       (!request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(['username']) ||
                        getAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/usernames/$(reservationKey(request.resource.data.username))).data.userId == request.auth.uid);

        // Nobody edits another member's profile from the client; nothing in
        // the app does. A role changes through the `setMemberRole` Cloud
        // Function, which refuses to leave a group with no admin (T034, T035).

        // No client delete. Leaving and removal both run in the
        // `removeUserFromGroup` Cloud Function, which releases the member's
        // names and clears their active group with it. A raw delete once left
        // the member's access behind in `users/{uid}.groups` (T080); since
        // T136 the profile itself is the membership, and it is still the
        // function's to delete.

        // Personal notes -- PRIVATE TO THEIR AUTHOR.
        // This block is new. Without it these documents fell through to the old
        // catch-all wildcard, which made every member's notes readable, listable
        // and writable by the entire group. The app's design has always treated
        // notes as personal (the client only ever reads its own uid's path);
        // this makes the rules agree with that design.
        //
        // Deliberately not readable by group admins either:
        // "private" that an admin can read is not private. If a future feature
        // lets a user SHARE a note, it should copy or publish it into a
        // campaign-scoped collection rather than widening this rule.
        //
        // A note is written only into a campaign that takes writes (T037,
        // DATA-010); it names its campaign in a field, not in its path.
        match /notes/{noteId} {
          allow read, delete: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId;
          allow create, update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId &&
                                 campaignOpen(groupId, request.resource.data.campaignId);
        }

        // Private records (T163): a player's people, places, quests and
        // rumours kept to themselves -- what happened in a one-to-one session
        // with the DM -- until they publish them. Owner-only, like notes:
        // not even an admin reads them. Shaped and capped as the campaign's
        // own records are, so publishing moves one across unchanged; writing
        // also requires membership and a campaign that takes writes. No
        // picture yet: a record's picture lives where every member reads it.
        match /private/{campaignId}/{entityCollection}/{entityId} {
          allow read, delete: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId &&
                               hasNotes(entityCollection);
          allow create: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId && isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                         hasNotes(entityCollection) && keepsItsName(entityCollection) &&
                         textFits(entityCollection) && stampsCreation() && holdsNoPicture() &&
                         campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId);
          allow update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId && isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                         hasNotes(entityCollection) && keepsItsName(entityCollection) &&
                         textFits(entityCollection) && stampsModification() && holdsNoPicture() &&
                         campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId);

          // A private record's notes, as a shared record's are (T133).
          match /notes/{noteId} {
            allow read, delete: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId;
            allow create: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId && isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                           hasNotes(entityCollection) && campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) &&
                           noteTextFits() && stampsCreation() &&
                           existsAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/users/$(userId)/private/$(campaignId)/$(entityCollection)/$(entityId));
            allow update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId && isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                           hasNotes(entityCollection) && campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) &&
                           noteTextFits() && stampsModification() &&
                           existsAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/users/$(userId)/private/$(campaignId)/$(entityCollection)/$(entityId));
          }
        }

        // A link held for a private record (T163): one whose owner is a
        // shared record, which must not carry a private record's id. Written
        // onto the owner when the record is published. Ids only, created and
        // deleted, never changed.
        match /private/{campaignId}/links/{linkId} {
          allow read, delete: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId;
          allow create: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId && isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                         campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) && heldLinkShape();
        }

        // A reader's place in each campaign's story, one document per campaign
        // (T073). Private for the same reason notes are: it records what one
        // player has read. It used to be one campaign-wide document every
        // member overwrote. Writing also requires membership, which the notes
        // rule does not: an owner-only check alone would let an account with
        // no invitation write into any group's subtree under its own uid.
        match /story-progress/{campaignId} {
          allow read: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId;
          allow delete: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId && isGroupMember(groupId);
          allow create, update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId && isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                                 campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId);
        }
      }

      // Username reservations in group
      match /usernames/{username} {
        // Allow username availability check for all (needed for registration)
        allow get: if true;

        // A member may reserve a name for themselves in their own group -- a
        // rename. Joining reserves the first one server-side, so a non-member
        // no longer needs to, and could previously squat names in any group.
        allow create: if isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                       request.resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;

        // Group members can list all usernames
        allow list: if isGroupMember(groupId);

        // A member may release their own reservation -- the other half of a
        // rename. Before 2026-09-23 only the global admin could, so every
        // rename by anyone else was denied (change 5). Nobody changes a
        // reservation, or releases someone else's, from the client.
        allow delete: if isSignedIn() && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;
      }

      // Group registration tokens
      match /registrationTokens/{invitationId} {
        // Allow token validation for everyone (needed for registration). The
        // document id is the token's SHA-256 (T145), so a `get` needs the
        // link, and a `list` yields no working one.
        allow get: if true;

        // Only group admins can list invitations
        allow list: if isGroupAdmin(groupId);

        // No client creates one (T145): `createInvitation` stores it under
        // its token's hash with the token sealed beside it. A browser-made
        // invitation would sit under a token anyone listing could read.

        // No client may mark a token used any more: `redeemInvitation` spends
        // it in the same transaction that grants membership. The rule this
        // replaces never required the token to be unused or unexpired.

        // A group admin names an invitation, and changes nothing else: not
        // `used`, which would bring a spent one back, nor `sealed` or `scheme`.
        allow update: if isGroupAdmin(groupId)
          && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['notes'])
          && request.resource.data.notes is string
          && request.resource.data.notes.size() <= 200;

        // Only group admins can delete tokens
        allow delete: if isGroupAdmin(groupId);
      }

      // Uploads whose document write may still be on its way (T084). The
      // client records one before it uploads and deletes it once the document
      // that points at the file is written; the daily sweep spares the file
      // while the entry is under 30 days old, then deletes both. The entry is
      // named after the file and stamped with the server's time, because it
      // is a lease: a client-chosen time could hold a file forever.
      match /pendingUploads/{uploadId} {
        allow create: if isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                       request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['path', 'uid', 'createdAt']) &&
                       request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid &&
                       request.resource.data.createdAt == request.time &&
                       request.resource.data.path is string &&
                       request.resource.data.path.matches('groups/' + groupId + '/.+/' + uploadId);

        // Only its owner removes it. Nobody reads or changes one: the sweep
        // reads them with the Admin SDK.
        allow delete: if isSignedIn() && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;
      }

      // An image file a document is about to stop pointing at (T084). The
      // client records it before the write that drops the image and deletes
      // the entry once it has deleted the file; the daily sweep deletes the
      // file of any entry left behind, once the document no longer points at
      // it. Same shape as `pendingUploads`, for the same reasons.
      match /releasedImages/{imageId} {
        allow create: if isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                       request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['path', 'uid', 'createdAt']) &&
                       request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid &&
                       request.resource.data.createdAt == request.time &&
                       request.resource.data.path is string &&
                       request.resource.data.path.matches('groups/' + groupId + '/.+/' + imageId);

        allow delete: if isSignedIn() && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;
      }

      // Campaigns and all campaign content.
      //
      // This replaces the previous `match /{collection}/{docId}` wildcard. That
      // wildcard also matched `users`, `usernames` and `registrationTokens`,
      // which is how a plain member could create registration tokens. Matching
      // `campaigns` explicitly means anything else under a group falls through
      // to the final deny rule instead of inheriting content permissions.
      match /campaigns/{campaignId} {
        allow read: if isGroupMember(groupId);
        // No client creates a campaign: the `createCampaign` Cloud Function
        // does, counting the group's campaigns in the same transaction, since
        // a group holds at most five (T128). Until then any member could, and
        // nothing bounded how many.
        allow create: if false;

        // RELAXED: any group member may edit campaign metadata -- not once
        // its deletion has started, and never the `deleting` mark (T037).
        allow update: if isGroupMember(groupId) && namedTextFits() &&
                       imageLeased(groupId, 'banner') &&
                       resource.data.get('deleting', false) != true &&
                       !request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(['deleting']);

        // Deleting a whole campaign stays admin-only -- it destroys every
        // entity and every member's notes for that campaign. See bug #1403 and
        // the deleteCampaign Cloud Function, which enforces the same check
        // server-side.
        allow delete: if isGroupAdmin(groupId);

        // Campaign records: npcs, locations, quests, rumors, chapters and the
        // saga, each named by its own field (`namingField`). Any other
        // collection is refused, read included (T119); a kind of record added
        // later needs its line there.
        match /{entityCollection}/{entityId} {
          allow read: if isRecordType(entityCollection) && isGroupMember(groupId);
          // `image` is an NPC's or a location's picture (T084); no other
          // entity carries one, so the check passes for them.
          // Only into a campaign that takes writes (T037, DATA-010).
          allow create: if isRecordType(entityCollection) && keepsItsName(entityCollection) &&
                         textFits(entityCollection) && stampsCreation() &&
                         leavesOldShapesEmpty(entityCollection) &&
                         isGroupMember(groupId) && imageLeased(groupId, 'image') &&
                         campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) &&
                         locationFenceHolds(groupId, campaignId, entityCollection);

          // RELAXED: previously `createdBy == uid || isGroupAdmin`, which the
          // UI never enforced, so members hit permission-denied on any entity
          // they had not personally created (bug #1406).
          allow update: if isRecordType(entityCollection) && keepsItsName(entityCollection) &&
                         textFits(entityCollection) &&
                         (stampsModification() || onlyRenumbersChapter(entityCollection)) &&
                         leavesOldShapesEmpty(entityCollection) &&
                         isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                         imageLeased(groupId, 'image') &&
                         campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) &&
                         locationFenceHolds(groupId, campaignId, entityCollection);

          // RELAXED: previously admin-only, which meant a member could not
          // delete even their OWN entity while the UI showed them a Delete
          // button.
          allow delete: if isRecordType(entityCollection) && isGroupMember(groupId);
        }

        // A record's notes, each a document of its own (T133): a person's,
        // a place's, a quest's (T163) and a rumour's. Their text is capped one note at a time,
        // which no rule could do inside the array they used to live in (F3,
        // F4). Any member may write them, as any member may the record, and
        // only under a record there is: after the write, so a new record's
        // note can commit with it (a combined rumour's).
        match /{entityCollection}/{entityId}/notes/{noteId} {
          allow read: if hasNotes(entityCollection) && isGroupMember(groupId);
          allow create: if hasNotes(entityCollection) && isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                         campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) && noteTextFits() && stampsCreation() &&
                         existsAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/campaigns/$(campaignId)/$(entityCollection)/$(entityId));
          allow update: if hasNotes(entityCollection) && isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                         campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) && noteTextFits() && stampsModification() &&
                         existsAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/campaigns/$(campaignId)/$(entityCollection)/$(entityId));
          allow delete: if hasNotes(entityCollection) && isGroupMember(groupId);
        }

        // A chapter's text, a document of its own beside the chapter (T134),
        // so the story's listener no longer downloads the book. It holds the
        // text alone, capped as the chapter's own was, and only under a
        // chapter there is after the write: a new chapter's text commits with it.
        match /chapters/{chapterId}/body/{bodyId} {
          allow read: if isGroupMember(groupId);
          allow create, update: if isGroupMember(groupId) && campaignOpen(groupId, campaignId) &&
                                 bodyId == 'text' && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['content']) &&
                                 fits(changedKeys(), 'content', 200000) &&
                                 existsAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/campaigns/$(campaignId)/chapters/$(chapterId));
          allow delete: if isGroupMember(groupId);
        }
      }
    }

    // Default deny rule
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
